un is not `duty` cycles.\n- Cause: `pwm <= (cnt <= duty_now)` is high for counter values 0..duty, which is duty+1 cycles.\n- Fix direction: use `<`.\n- Repro: tb/tb_pwm_pulse_width.v. It holds duty=3 and measures three high runs, expecting 3 each.\n\nBucket C, duty update timing (duty_update_fail.log)\n- Signature: after a mid-period duty change, the current period's rise-to-rise interval is shorter than the period.\n- Cause: `wire [3:0] duty_now = duty;` drives the compare from the live input. The sampled duty_q is written but never used, so a change applies immediately instead of at the next period.\n- Fix direction: compare against the value sampled at cnt==0, for example `(cnt == 0) ? duty : duty_q`.\n- Repro: tb/tb_pwm_duty_update.v. It runs at duty=2 and raises duty to 6 just after the falling edge. It expects the low phase to complete the period (high + low == measured period) and the next high run to be wider.\n\nIndependence of the repros: each repro measures its behaviour relative to observed pwm edges and does not use absolute phase. My expectation is that each one passes on RTL that has only one of the other two bugs. The scorer requires that each seeded bug fail a different repro, and the repros were written with that in mind. I have not run them, and the platform's simulator.run gate will decide.\n\nOpen points:\n- Rerun each log through veritriage.investigate on its own, as the procedure recommends, to check the log-to-bucket mapping.\n- Confirm that each failure repeats with the same seed and build before closing as RTL bugs."}], "files": [{"path": "tb/tb_pwm_period.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Repro A: pwm period must be PERIOD cycles", "summary": "Self-checking repro for bucket A against [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. It holds duty=3 and checks three rise-to-rise intervals equal 8. The counter wrap at PERIOD-2 should make each interval 7, so these checks are expected to fail. It prints the nonce verdict once before $finish.", "entry": "tb_pwm_period"}, {"path": "tb/tb_pwm_pulse_width.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Repro B: pwm high time must be duty cycles", "summary": "Self-checking repro for bucket B against [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. It holds duty=3 and checks three high runs equal 3 cycles. The compare `cnt <= duty_now` should give 4, so these checks are expected to fail. It prints the nonce verdict once before $finish.", "entry": "tb_pwm_pulse_width"}, {"path": "tb/tb_pwm_duty_update.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Repro C: mid-period duty change must take effect next period", "summary": "Self-checking repro for bucket C against [artifact:prj-f58c6d66a9.design-under-investigation.a1]. It runs at duty=2, measures the period, and changes duty to 6 right after the falling edge. It then checks that high+low of the current period equals the period and that the next high run is wider. Because the RTL compares against live duty, pwm should go high again in the same period. It prints the nonce verdict once before $finish.", "entry": "tb_pwm_duty_update"}], "tool_runs": ["run:run-0001"], "claims": ["Mapping of log files to buckets is inferred from the log file names and the RTL reading, not from per-log VeriTriage sessions.", "Each repro is expected to fail on the RTL under investigation and to pass on RTL carrying only one of the other two bugs; this has not been simulated by me.", "The intended clean behaviour is taken from the RTL header comment; the reference clean RTL was not available to this seat.", "The failures have not been rerun with the same seed and build to rule out flakiness."], "escalation": null, "notes": "The VeriTriage result is low confidence (30%, unknown_failure), and the buckets rest on reading the RTL against its documented intent, so uncertainty is set at 0.3. Recommended follow-up: run veritriage.investigate on each log separately. Each testbench bounds its wait loops at 64 cycles, so every run reaches $finish and print